the GMB Union has announced that more  than ten thousand ambulance workers will  go on strike for two days later this  month they'll walk out on December the  21st and the 28th or joining me now is  our correspondent Saba Chaudhary who's  at the GMB headquarters in central  London uh many many organizations  striking at the moment Saba but this  will cause real worry for those who may  be depending on ambulances to get to  appointments at hospital or indeed those  who may have emergencies on that day  good afternoon it really is looking like  a Christmas period full of strike action  just last night it was the rmt union  which represents train workers saying  that they will be striking over the  Christmas period and today this Union  over here the GMB which represents some  ambulance workers are saying that they  will be walking out on the 21st and the  28th of December later this month now it  was just last week that the GMB Union at  10 000 of their members voted in favor  of walking out impacting nine of their  trusts across England and Wales and  they're saying that it essentially boils  down to money they're not happy with the  four percent pay increase that the  government have offered their workers  they're saying that it's tantamount to a  pay cut given inflation and the cost of  living crisis and that it's simply  unsatisfactory the members of the GMB  Union have also said though it's not  simply about money it's massive  contributing factor but it's also other  things specifically the shortage of  staff that they're seeing at the moment  and also the number of the the patient's  safety issue is something that is is  also a a secondary issue coming from  staff shortages now around a third of  the members have said that it's for  different factors including delays and  cuts that they believe uh that is the  contributing factor for why some of  their patients have died a third of  members said that now we're looking at  widespread delays as I say throughout  the Christmas period but their message  here is loud and clear they are on their  knees they say and unless the government  give in to their demands and hear them  out it's going to look like a very  difficult Christmas indeed
 

